Third-party code and derived knowledge
--------------------------------------

1. `tools/wb_desktop_session.cjs`
   Derived from `maureen2215-ai/workbuddy-auto-claim-public` (MIT), a community
   project. The decryption contract (using the client's own Electron binary as
   the decryptor instead of extracting its key) is that project's idea; the file
   header records what was changed. It contains no secret.

2. API and enum descriptions in `docs/mechanisms.md` and the provider module
   docstrings were obtained by reading the shipped JavaScript of the respective
   desktop clients on a machine the author owns, and by observing the requests
   those clients make. No proprietary source file is reproduced in this
   repository — constants (endpoint paths, field names, status-code enums) are
   documented because they are needed to interoperate, which is the same
   approach any API client takes.

3. `keeper/providers/qoder_cn.py` uses Chromium/Electron's own on-disk storage
   format (`v10` AES-GCM container + Windows DPAPI) to read a session that the
   logged-in client created for *this Windows user*. Nothing is decrypted that
   the user could not already read from their own profile.

Usage etiquette
---------------

* This tool claims rewards that the vendor publishes for *your own* account, at
  the rate the vendor allows, using the credential already stored on your
  machine. It does not create accounts, bypass payment, farm other people's
  accounts, or defeat rate limits — the retry design deliberately spreads
  attempts across a few scheduled slots per day instead of hammering.
* Check each vendor's terms yourself. Promotional campaigns change or disappear
  without notice; several of the enums and parameter sets here were observed in
  October 2026 and may already be stale. If a vendor asks that this stop, stop.
* Do not commit `config.json`, logs or state files: they name your accounts. The
  default `.gitignore` keeps them out of git.
